      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-103811",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The domain of the function is all positive real numbers.\n- $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, the statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$ Hence, statement III is true."
    },
